# React Component Construction Rules

This file contains guidelines for building maintainable and performant React components in this project.

## Component Structure

### File Organization

- One component per file with the same name as the file (PascalCase)
- Use default exports for components
- Place related types/interfaces in the same file or in `src/entities/`
- Co-locate component-specific utilities in the same directory

```typescript
// ✓ Good
export default function UserProfile({ user }: UserProfileProps) {
  // Component implementation
}

// ✗ Avoid
export const UserProfile = ({ user }: UserProfileProps) => {
  // Component implementation
}
```

### Component Anatomy Order

Organize component code in this order:

1. Type definitions (interfaces, types)
2. Constants and configuration
3. Custom hooks
4. Helper functions
5. Main component function
6. Sub-components (if small and tightly coupled)

```typescript
// 1. Types
interface UserCardProps {
  user: User;
  onSelect?: (id: string) => void;
}

// 2. Constants
const DEFAULT_AVATAR = '/images/default-avatar.png';

// 3. Helper functions
function formatUserName(user: User): string {
  return `${user.firstName} ${user.lastName}`;
}

// 4. Main component
export default function UserCard({ user, onSelect }: UserCardProps) {
  // Component logic
}
```

## Performance Optimization

### Memoization

- Use `React.memo()` for components that re-render frequently with the same props
- Use `useMemo()` for expensive calculations
- Use `useCallback()` for functions passed as props to memoized children
- Don't over-optimize - measure before memoizing

```typescript
// ✓ Good - Memoize expensive list items
const UserListItem = React.memo(function UserListItem({ user }: { user: User }) {
  return <div>{user.name}</div>;
});

// ✓ Good - Memoize expensive calculations
function UserStats({ users }: { users: User[] }) {
  const statistics = useMemo(() => {
    return calculateComplexStatistics(users);
  }, [users]);

  return <div>{statistics.total}</div>;
}

// ✓ Good - Memoize callbacks for memoized children
function UserList({ users }: { users: User[] }) {
  const handleSelect = useCallback((id: string) => {
    console.log('Selected:', id);
  }, []);

  return users.map((user) => (
    <UserListItem key={user.id} user={user} onSelect={handleSelect} />
  ));
}
```

### Avoid Inline Functions and Objects

- Don't create new function or object references in render
- Extract to constants or use memoization hooks

```typescript
// ✗ Avoid - Creates new function on every render
function UserCard({ user }: { user: User }) {
  return <Button onClick={() => console.log(user.id)}>Click</Button>;
}

// ✓ Good - Use useCallback
function UserCard({ user }: { user: User }) {
  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
    console.log(user.id);
  }, [user.id]);

  return <Button onClick={handleClick}>Click</Button>;
}

// ✗ Avoid - Creates new object on every render
<div style={{ marginTop: 10, color: 'blue' }} />

// ✓ Good - Extract to constant
const cardStyle = { marginTop: 10, color: 'blue' };
<div style={cardStyle} />
```

### List Rendering

- Always use stable, unique keys (prefer IDs over indexes)
- Memoize list items to prevent unnecessary re-renders
- Use virtualization for long lists (react-window or @tanstack/react-virtual)

```typescript
// ✓ Good - Stable unique keys
function UserList({ users }: { users: User[] }) {
  return users.map((user) => (
    <UserCard key={user.id} user={user} />
  ));
}

// ✗ Avoid - Index as key
function UserList({ users }: { users: User[] }) {
  return users.map((user, index) => (
    <UserCard key={index} user={user} />
  ));
}
```

## State Management

### Local State

- Keep state as local as possible
- Use `useState` for simple values
- Use `useReducer` for complex state logic
- Initialize state with functions for expensive computations

```typescript
// ✓ Good - Lazy initialization
const [data, setData] = useState(() => {
  return parseExpensiveData(rawData);
});

// ✗ Avoid - Runs on every render
const [data, setData] = useState(parseExpensiveData(rawData));
```

### State Updates

- Use functional updates when new state depends on previous state
- Batch related state updates together
- Avoid unnecessary state - derive values when possible

```typescript
// ✓ Good - Functional update
setCount((prevCount) => prevCount + 1);

// ✗ Avoid - May use stale state
setCount(count + 1);

// ✓ Good - Derive values
function UserCard({ user }: { user: User }) {
  const displayName = `${user.firstName} ${user.lastName}`;
  return <div>{displayName}</div>;
}

// ✗ Avoid - Unnecessary state
function UserCard({ user }: { user: User }) {
  const [displayName, setDisplayName] = useState('');
  
  useEffect(() => {
    setDisplayName(`${user.firstName} ${user.lastName}`);
  }, [user]);
  
  return <div>{displayName}</div>;
}
```

### Global State

- Use Zustand for global application state
- Use SWR for server state and data fetching
- Keep global state minimal - prefer composition and props

## Effects and Side Effects

### useEffect Best Practices

- Keep effects focused on a single purpose
- Always include all dependencies in the dependency array
- Clean up side effects properly
- Use separate effects for unrelated side effects

```typescript
// ✓ Good - Focused effect with cleanup
useEffect(() => {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  
  async function fetchData() {
    try {
      const response = await fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal });
      const data = await response.json();
      setData(data);
    } catch (error) {
      if (error.name !== 'AbortError') {
        setError(error);
      }
    }
  }
  
  fetchData();
  
  return () => controller.abort();
}, [url]);

// ✗ Avoid - Multiple unrelated side effects
useEffect(() => {
  fetchUserData();
  trackPageView();
  updateDocumentTitle();
}, [user, page, title]);

// ✓ Good - Separate effects
useEffect(() => {
  fetchUserData();
}, [user]);

useEffect(() => {
  trackPageView();
}, [page]);

useEffect(() => {
  updateDocumentTitle();
}, [title]);
```

### Avoid useEffect When Not Needed

- Don't use effects to transform data - do it during render
- Don't use effects to reset state on prop changes - use keys or derived state
- Prefer event handlers over effects when responding to user actions

```typescript
// ✗ Avoid - Unnecessary effect for data transformation
function UserProfile({ user }: { user: User }) {
  const [fullName, setFullName] = useState('');
  
  useEffect(() => {
    setFullName(`${user.firstName} ${user.lastName}`);
  }, [user]);
  
  return <div>{fullName}</div>;
}

// ✓ Good - Transform during render
function UserProfile({ user }: { user: User }) {
  const fullName = `${user.firstName} ${user.lastName}`;
  return <div>{fullName}</div>;
}

// ✗ Avoid - Using effect to handle user interaction
function SearchInput() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  
  useEffect(() => {
    if (query) {
      performSearch(query);
    }
  }, [query]);
  
  return <input value={query} onChange={(e) => setQuery(e.target.value)} />;
}

// ✓ Good - Use event handler
function SearchInput() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  
  const handleSearch = (e: FormEvent) => {
    e.preventDefault();
    performSearch(query);
  };
  
  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSearch}>
      <input value={query} onChange={(e) => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
    </form>
  );
}
```

## Custom Hooks

### Hook Design

- Extract reusable logic into custom hooks
- Prefix custom hooks with "use"
- Return values in a consistent format (object or array)
- Keep hooks focused on a single concern

```typescript
// ✓ Good - Focused, reusable hook
function useLocalStorage<T>(key: string, initialValue: T) {
  const [value, setValue] = useState<T>(() => {
    try {
      const item = window.localStorage.getItem(key);
      return item ? JSON.parse(item) : initialValue;
    } catch {
      return initialValue;
    }
  });

  const setStoredValue = useCallback((newValue: T) => {
    try {
      setValue(newValue);
      window.localStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify(newValue));
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Failed to save to localStorage:', error);
    }
  }, [key]);

  return [value, setStoredValue] as const;
}

// ✓ Good - Hook for data fetching
function useUser(userId: string) {
  const { data, error, isLoading } = useSWR(
    `/api/users/${userId}`,
    fetcher
  );

  return {
    user: data,
    isLoading,
    isError: error
  };
}
```

## Props and Types

### Prop Design

- Keep prop interfaces focused and minimal
- Use optional props with default values when appropriate
- Prefer explicit types over generic types when possible
- Document complex props with JSDoc comments

```typescript
// ✓ Good - Clear, focused props
interface ButtonProps {
  /** Button label text */
  children: React.ReactNode;
  /** Click event handler */
  onClick?: () => void;
  /** Visual style variant */
  variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary' | 'ghost';
  /** Disabled state */
  disabled?: boolean;
  /** Full width button */
  fullWidth?: boolean;
}

export default function Button({
  children,
  onClick,
  variant = 'primary',
  disabled = false,
  fullWidth = false
}: ButtonProps) {
  // Implementation
}
```

### Prop Destructuring

- Destructure props in function parameter for clarity
- Use default values in destructuring
- Don't destructure if you need the entire props object

```typescript
// ✓ Good - Clear destructuring with defaults
function Button({ label, onClick, variant = 'primary' }: ButtonProps) {
  return <button onClick={onClick}>{label}</button>;
}

// ✗ Avoid - Accessing props.x repeatedly
function Button(props: ButtonProps) {
  return <button onClick={props.onClick}>{props.label}</button>;
}
```

### Children Prop

- Use `React.ReactNode` for children that can be any renderable content
- Use `React.ReactElement` when you need a specific component
- Consider render props or slots pattern for complex children

```typescript
// ✓ Good - Flexible children
interface CardProps {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  title: string;
}

// ✓ Good - Specific component type
interface ModalProps {
  children: React.ReactElement<DialogProps>;
}

// ✓ Good - Render prop pattern
interface DataTableProps<T> {
  data: T[];
  renderRow: (item: T) => React.ReactNode;
}
```

## Component Composition

### Composition Over Props

- Use composition to build flexible components
- Prefer component composition over conditional rendering props
- Use compound components for related UI elements

```typescript
// ✗ Avoid - Boolean props for variations
interface CardProps {
  showHeader?: boolean;
  showFooter?: boolean;
  headerContent?: React.ReactNode;
  footerContent?: React.ReactNode;
}

// ✓ Good - Composition pattern
interface CardProps {
  children: React.ReactNode;
}

function Card({ children }: CardProps) {
  return <div className="card">{children}</div>;
}

Card.Header = function CardHeader({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return <div className="card-header">{children}</div>;
};

Card.Body = function CardBody({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return <div className="card-body">{children}</div>;
};

Card.Footer = function CardFooter({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return <div className="card-footer">{children}</div>;
};

// Usage
<Card>
  <Card.Header>Title</Card.Header>
  <Card.Body>Content</Card.Body>
  <Card.Footer>Actions</Card.Footer>
</Card>
```

### Container/Presentational Pattern

- Separate data fetching (container) from presentation (presentational)
- Keep presentational components pure when possible
- Makes components easier to test and reuse

```typescript
// Presentational component
interface UserListProps {
  users: User[];
  onSelectUser: (id: string) => void;
  isLoading: boolean;
}

function UserList({ users, onSelectUser, isLoading }: UserListProps) {
  if (isLoading) return <div>Loading...</div>;
  
  return (
    <div>
      {users.map((user) => (
        <UserCard key={user.id} user={user} onSelect={onSelectUser} />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}

// Container component
function UserListContainer() {
  const { data: users, isLoading } = useUsers();
  const handleSelectUser = useCallback((id: string) => {
    // Handle selection
  }, []);
  
  return (
    <UserList
      users={users ?? []}
      onSelectUser={handleSelectUser}
      isLoading={isLoading}
    />
  );
}
```

## Error Handling

### Error Boundaries

- Use Error Boundaries to catch rendering errors
- Provide meaningful fallback UI
- Log errors for monitoring

```typescript
// ✓ Good - Error boundary with fallback
import { ErrorBoundary } from 'react-error-boundary';

function ErrorFallback({ error }: { error: Error }) {
  return (
    <div role="alert">
      <h2>Something went wrong</h2>
      <pre>{error.message}</pre>
    </div>
  );
}

function App() {
  return (
    <ErrorBoundary FallbackComponent={ErrorFallback}>
      <UserProfile />
    </ErrorBoundary>
  );
}
```

### Async Error Handling

- Handle errors in async operations gracefully
- Provide user feedback for errors
- Don't let errors crash the app

```typescript
// ✓ Good - Proper error handling
function UserProfile({ userId }: { userId: string }) {
  const { user, isLoading, error } = useUser(userId);
  
  if (error) {
    return <ErrorMessage message="Failed to load user profile" />;
  }
  
  if (isLoading) {
    return <LoadingSpinner />;
  }
  
  return <div>{user.name}</div>;
}
```

## Accessibility

### Semantic HTML

- Use semantic HTML elements
- Include proper ARIA attributes when needed
- Ensure keyboard navigation works

```typescript
// ✓ Good - Semantic and accessible
function Dialog({ isOpen, onClose, title, children }: DialogProps) {
  return (
    <dialog
      open={isOpen}
      aria-labelledby="dialog-title"
      aria-modal="true"
    >
      <h2 id="dialog-title">{title}</h2>
      <div>{children}</div>
      <button onClick={onClose} aria-label="Close dialog">
        ×
      </button>
    </dialog>
  );
}
```

### Focus Management

- Manage focus for modals and dynamic content
- Ensure focus indicators are visible
- Support keyboard navigation

```typescript
// ✓ Good - Focus management
function Modal({ isOpen, onClose, children }: ModalProps) {
  const modalRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);
  
  useEffect(() => {
    if (isOpen && modalRef.current) {
      modalRef.current.focus();
    }
  }, [isOpen]);
  
  return (
    <div
      ref={modalRef}
      tabIndex={-1}
      role="dialog"
      aria-modal="true"
    >
      {children}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Testing Considerations

### Write Testable Components

- Keep components pure when possible
- Extract complex logic to utility functions
- Avoid tight coupling to external dependencies
- Use dependency injection for testability

```typescript
// ✓ Good - Testable component
interface UserCardProps {
  user: User;
  onDelete?: (id: string) => void;
  formatDate?: (date: Date) => string;
}

export default function UserCard({
  user,
  onDelete,
  formatDate = defaultFormatDate
}: UserCardProps) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h3>{user.name}</h3>
      <p>{formatDate(user.createdAt)}</p>
      {onDelete && (
        <button onClick={() => onDelete(user.id)}>Delete</button>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Code Style Consistency

### Naming Conventions

- Components: PascalCase (`UserProfile`, `DataTable`)
- Props interfaces: ComponentName + Props (`UserProfileProps`)
- Event handlers: handle + EventName (`handleClick`, `handleSubmit`)
- Boolean props/state: is/has/should prefix (`isLoading`, `hasError`, `shouldShow`)

### Conditional Rendering

- Use ternary for if-else conditions
- Use && for simple conditional rendering
- Extract complex conditions to variables

```typescript
// ✓ Good - Clear conditional rendering
function UserStatus({ user }: { user: User }) {
  const isActive = user.status === 'active' && user.lastSeen > Date.now() - 3600000;
  
  return (
    <div>
      {isActive ? <ActiveBadge /> : <InactiveBadge />}
      {user.isPremium && <PremiumBadge />}
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Component Documentation

- Add JSDoc comments for complex components
- Document non-obvious behavior
- Include usage examples for shared components

```typescript
/**
 * UserProfile displays comprehensive user information with avatar,
 * stats, and action buttons.
 * 
 * @example
 * ```tsx
 * <UserProfile
 *   user={currentUser}
 *   onEdit={handleEdit}
 *   showStats
 * />
 * ```
 */
export default function UserProfile({ user, onEdit, showStats }: UserProfileProps) {
  // Implementation
}
```

## Performance Monitoring

- Use React DevTools Profiler to identify performance issues
- Measure before optimizing
- Monitor bundle size impact of component dependencies
- Use code splitting for large components

```typescript
// ✓ Good - Lazy load heavy components
const HeavyChart = lazy(() => import('./HeavyChart'));

function Dashboard() {
  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<ChartSkeleton />}>
      <HeavyChart data={data} />
    </Suspense>
  );
}
```

## Summary Checklist

Before committing a component, verify:

- [ ] Component has a single, clear responsibility
- [ ] Props are minimal and well-typed
- [ ] State is as local as possible
- [ ] Effects have proper cleanup
- [ ] Memoization is used appropriately (not over-optimized)
- [ ] List items have stable keys
- [ ] Error states are handled
- [ ] Component is accessible (semantic HTML, ARIA, keyboard nav)
- [ ] Code follows project style guidelines
- [ ] Complex logic has comments/documentation
- [ ] Component can be easily tested
